lesson g1-taking-away-to-twenty v=1 format=worked grade=1 unit=2 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Taking away to twenty"
  goal "Take away by counting back, compare two amounts, and work backwards to where a story started."

  example {
    worked sub.bus-off n=10 off=4 easy-level=medium hard-level=medium
    say """
      Ten on the bus and four get off. Count back from ten: nine, eight, seven, six. Six are left.
      """
  }
  exercises {
    practice sub.bus-off count=2 seed=2
    practice sub.towers-difference count=2 seed=3
    practice sub.fewer-than count=1 seed=6
    practice sub.take-from-ten count=1 seed=4
    practice sub.plate-ate count=1 seed=3
    practice sub.ants-start count=1 seed=8
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show sub.bus-how-many-off n=11 left=4 easy-n=8 easy-left=5 hard-n=12 hard-left=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show sub.work-back a=3 b=4 c=5 easy-a=2 easy-b=3 easy-c=2 hard-a=5 hard-c=8
  }

  grown-ups """
    Counting back is harder to say than counting on, so let the child use fingers while they say the
    numbers. How many more is also a taking away, even though nothing leaves: the towers show it as
    the part of one tower that sticks up above the other. Taking away past ten goes in two steps:
    the loose counters first, then the rest from the full frame. The biscuits and the ants put the
    unknown in the middle and at the start. The three-star question goes backwards through two
    stops, and acting it out with toys is a good way in.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the bus holds under ten, the towers are close in height, the take-aways only just cross ten, and the work back to the start stays under ten."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the bus starts with twelve, the differences reach eight, and the work back to the start goes past fifteen."
  }
}
